\begin{remark}[Physics notation: renormalised operators and their transformations]
$M(\varepsilon)$ is the bare operator with its counterterms, $\psi$ the finite part that fixes the renormalisation scheme of the composite operators ($\psi=0$ is minimal subtraction of the singular terms $\varepsilon^{-m}\log^k\varepsilon$), and $M[T]$ the renormalised operator in the Wilsonian theory at scale $T$. $d_T$ is the quantum BRST differential at scale $T$, and $L(M)$ the local composite operator representing the quantum transformation of $M$, unique by Theorem \ref{thm:c8-L} (i). The cohomology of $\Lambda^{(0)}$ on local functionals is the space of physical local operators (BRST-closed modulo BRST-exact), and by Corollary \ref{cor:c8-homotopy} $x\mapsto-\Lambda^{(1)}(x)$ is the action of the superconformal algebra on it, closed up to BRST-exact terms; through it the physics dilatation $D=-E=-\tfrac12\Delta$ acts on an operator of degree $0$ at the origin as multiplication by its scaling dimension $w_O+\gamma_O$.
\end{remark}

